1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,101
This video is about regular expressions. A lexer uses them to describe its tokens.

2
00:00:06,551 --> 00:00:19,287
We learn the operators, the letters and the precedence. Then we write regexes for tokens. At the end we solve the two exercises from the slides and three past midterm questions.

3
00:00:19,737 --> 00:00:23,684
A lexer reads the input bytes and cuts them into tokens.

4
00:00:24,134 --> 00:00:30,726
This input is cut into four tokens: a keyword, an identifier, a relation and an integer.

5
00:00:31,176 --> 00:00:37,555
Each token class is a set of strings. The keyword set has three members: if, else and while.

6
00:00:38,005 --> 00:00:43,722
The identifier set has many members: x, x 1, count, y 2 k, and more.

7
00:00:44,172 --> 00:00:50,935
The integer set also has many members: 0, 7, 42, 0 0 7, and more.

8
00:00:51,385 --> 00:00:58,873
The keyword set is finite. The identifier set and the integer set are infinite. We cannot list their members.

9
00:00:59,323 --> 00:01:06,555
We need a finite description that is exact. A regular expression is that description. It is easy to write.

10
00:01:07,005 --> 00:01:11,250
And it is simple enough that a recognizer can be generated from it.

11
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,948
The recognizer answers yes or no for each string w: is w in the set?

12
00:01:17,398 --> 00:01:28,854
There are many formalisms for tokens. Regular languages are the most popular. The theory is simple, they are easy to understand, and the implementations are efficient.

13
00:01:29,304 --> 00:01:40,589
A regular expression is built over an alphabet. Three operations join small regexes into bigger ones. The first is concatenation: A, then B.

14
00:01:41,039 --> 00:01:44,901
Example: the regex a b matches only the string a b.

15
00:01:45,351 --> 00:01:48,017
The second is alternation: A or B.

16
00:01:48,467 --> 00:01:52,755
Example: the regex a or b matches a, and it matches b.

17
00:01:53,205 --> 00:01:58,517
The third is repetition, also called the star: zero or more copies of A.

18
00:01:58,967 --> 00:02:04,087
Example: a star matches the empty string, a, a a, a a a, and so on.

19
00:02:04,537 --> 00:02:15,353
The course notes say that regular expressions describe exactly what a finite automaton can compute. Lectures 4 and 5 show how to build that automaton.

20
00:02:15,803 --> 00:02:26,107
Slide 26 gives the syntax of a regex as a grammar. The simplest regex is one letter. The letter a matches exactly the string a.

21
00:02:26,557 --> 00:02:34,259
Parentheses group a regex, so an operator can apply to all of it. Open, a b, close matches a b.

22
00:02:34,709 --> 00:02:43,605
Three operators come after a regex. Question mark means zero or one. a question mark matches the empty string, or a.

23
00:02:44,055 --> 00:02:49,217
Plus means one or more: a, a a, a a a, and so on. The set is infinite.

24
00:02:49,667 --> 00:02:55,491
Star means zero or more. It adds the empty string. This set is infinite too.

25
00:02:55,941 --> 00:03:00,827
Two regexes side by side mean sequence. a b matches a b.

26
00:03:01,277 --> 00:03:07,741
A bar between two regexes means choice. a or b matches a, and it matches b.

27
00:03:08,191 --> 00:03:17,001
Precedence: question mark, plus and star bind tightest. Then sequence. Then the bar. Use parentheses when you are not sure.

28
00:03:17,451 --> 00:03:28,395
Slide 27 defines a letter. A letter always matches exactly one character. We test five input characters: a, b, d, 7 and a space.

29
00:03:28,845 --> 00:03:34,371
A plain character is an exact match. The letter a matches only the character a.

30
00:03:34,821 --> 00:03:40,538
A dot matches any character. It matches all five input characters, the space too.

31
00:03:40,988 --> 00:03:48,689
Square brackets list a group. Bracket a b c matches a and b here. It does not match d, 7 or the space.

32
00:03:49,139 --> 00:03:57,118
A caret after the bracket inverts the group. It matches any character that is not in the group: d, 7 and the space.

33
00:03:57,568 --> 00:04:05,525
A dash inside the brackets gives a range. Bracket zero dash nine is any digit. Here it matches only 7.

34
00:04:05,975 --> 00:04:15,618
Bracket capital A to Z, small a to z, is a range of 26 capital letters and 26 small letters. That is 52 letters.

35
00:04:16,068 --> 00:04:22,425
Precedence tells you which operator applies to which part. Take the regex a b star or c.

36
00:04:22,875 --> 00:04:27,995
Star binds tightest. So the star applies only to b, not to a b.

37
00:04:28,445 --> 00:04:31,901
Next comes sequence. a is followed by b star.

38
00:04:32,351 --> 00:04:38,197
The bar binds weakest. So the whole regex is a choice: a b star, or c.

39
00:04:38,647 --> 00:04:42,423
The tree shows the same structure. The bar is at the root.

40
00:04:42,873 --> 00:04:46,563
So the regex matches a, a b, a b b, and c.

41
00:04:47,013 --> 00:04:48,805
It does not match a c.

42
00:04:49,255 --> 00:04:54,141
Parentheses change the tree. a, then b star or c, matches a c.

43
00:04:54,591 --> 00:04:59,647
But it does not match c alone. If you are not sure, write the parentheses.

44
00:05:00,097 --> 00:05:07,307
Slide 28 is an exercise. Each row gives a set of strings. You write a regex for each set.

45
00:05:07,757 --> 00:05:15,992
The first row is done. a b c matches only the string a b c. It is a sequence of three one-letter regexes.

46
00:05:16,442 --> 00:05:18,085
Pause the video and try it.

47
00:05:18,535 --> 00:05:21,649
The set a b, a b c. The c is optional.

48
00:05:22,099 --> 00:05:28,585
So write a b, then c question mark. The parentheses show what the question mark applies to.

49
00:05:29,035 --> 00:05:32,213
The set a b c, a c. Now b is optional.

50
00:05:32,663 --> 00:05:35,095
So write a, b question mark, c.

51
00:05:35,545 --> 00:05:41,177
The set a, a b, a b b, and so on. The dots mean that the set is infinite.

52
00:05:41,627 --> 00:05:45,574
Write a, then b star. Star means zero or more b.

53
00:05:46,024 --> 00:05:49,779
Now the last three sets. The first four rows are done.

54
00:05:50,229 --> 00:05:53,557
The empty string, a b, a b a b, and so on.

55
00:05:54,007 --> 00:06:00,343
The star must apply to a b. So put a b in parentheses: open, a b, close, star.

56
00:06:00,793 --> 00:06:05,273
The set a b, a b b, a b b b. There is at least one b.

57
00:06:05,723 --> 00:06:08,752
This is a b plus. Plus means one or more.

58
00:06:09,202 --> 00:06:13,703
It is the same as a b b star: one copy of b, then star.

59
00:06:14,153 --> 00:06:15,796
The set if, else, while.

60
00:06:16,246 --> 00:06:21,707
Use choice: if, or else, or while. One regex covers all the keywords.

61
00:06:22,157 --> 00:06:28,067
A common mistake is to read a star as: a, then anything. Take the input a b c.

62
00:06:28,517 --> 00:06:32,143
Match it against a star. The first character a matches.

63
00:06:32,593 --> 00:06:36,604
Then b is not a, so a star cannot continue. No match.

64
00:06:37,054 --> 00:06:42,494
To say a, then any number of any symbols, write a dot star. Again the a matches.

65
00:06:42,944 --> 00:06:46,635
The dot matches b, and the dot matches c. Match.

66
00:06:47,085 --> 00:06:53,911
So a star matches only strings of a: the empty string, a, a a a. It does not match a b c.

67
00:06:54,361 --> 00:06:59,801
a dot star matches every string that starts with a. b a does not start with a.

68
00:07:00,251 --> 00:07:08,337
Plus is not a new power. A plus is A followed by A star. So a b plus is the same set as a b b star.

69
00:07:08,787 --> 00:07:14,845
Question mark is a choice with the empty string. A question mark is A or epsilon.

70
00:07:15,295 --> 00:07:21,247
A bracket group is a choice of single letters. Bracket a b c is a or b or c.

71
00:07:21,697 --> 00:07:27,116
A range is a long choice: 0 or 1 or 2, and so on up to 9.

72
00:07:27,566 --> 00:07:30,083
The dot is a choice of every character.

73
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:38,149
All these extras reduce to the three base operations. They add no power. They only make the regex shorter.

74
00:07:38,599 --> 00:07:48,925
Slide 30 gives regexes for numbers. A single digit is bracket zero to nine. It matches 7. It does not match 42 or x.

75
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:57,183
A non-digit uses the caret: any one character that is not a digit. x and the space match. 5 does not.

76
00:07:57,633 --> 00:08:05,782
A natural number is one or more digits: bracket zero to nine, plus. 42 and 0 0 7 match.

77
00:08:06,232 --> 00:08:11,181
The empty string is not a natural number. Minus 7 is not one either.

78
00:08:11,631 --> 00:08:21,061
An integer adds an optional minus sign in front: bracket minus, question mark. So 7 and minus 0 0 7 match.

79
00:08:21,511 --> 00:08:26,460
A plus sign is not allowed, and a minus sign alone is not an integer.

80
00:08:26,910 --> 00:08:35,827
A decimal is a natural number, then an optional part: a point and one or more digits. So 3 and 3 point 14 match.

81
00:08:36,277 --> 00:08:39,435
3 point alone, and point 5, do not match.

82
00:08:39,885 --> 00:08:48,631
The point is written with a backslash. Without the backslash, the dot matches any character. Then 3 x 14 would be a decimal.

83
00:08:49,081 --> 00:08:58,511
Slide 30 also gives a regex for a phone number. Here it is, next to the number 5 5 5, dash, 1 2 3 4.

84
00:08:58,961 --> 00:09:01,222
First, a digit from 1 to 9.

85
00:09:01,672 --> 00:09:03,037
Then two digits.

86
00:09:03,487 --> 00:09:04,639
Then a dash.

87
00:09:05,089 --> 00:09:06,561
Then four digits.

88
00:09:07,011 --> 00:09:14,009
The first digit cannot be zero. So 0 5 5, dash, 1 2 3 4 does not match.

89
00:09:14,459 --> 00:09:16,805
The second form on the slide is shorter.

90
00:09:17,255 --> 00:09:27,901
Backslash d means a digit. Brace 2 means exactly two copies. These are extras of the Java regex library. They are short forms too.

91
00:09:28,351 --> 00:09:35,924
The slide says that the spaces are optional. That is the job of the two parts: backslash b, question mark.

92
00:09:36,374 --> 00:09:41,835
But in Java, backslash b is a word boundary. It does not match any character.

93
00:09:42,285 --> 00:09:53,187
Our check: the regex matches 5 5 5, dash, 1 2 3 4. It rejects 5 5 5, space, dash, space, 1 2 3 4.

94
00:09:53,637 --> 00:10:00,122
To allow an optional space, write a space followed by a question mark. Then both forms match.

95
00:10:00,572 --> 00:10:12,263
The course notes use grep as an example. Grep looks like a search program. But it is a compiler: its source program is a regex, and it builds a finite state machine.

96
00:10:12,713 --> 00:10:22,014
But grep returns the matching lines, not the machine. So it is more like an interpreter: it evaluates the regex on a given text.

97
00:10:22,464 --> 00:10:36,331
Most accurate: grep is a just in time compiler. It builds the machine, runs it on the text, returns the matches, and then discards the machine. Lectures 4 and 5 show how to build this machine.

98
00:10:36,781 --> 00:10:44,482
Java has a regex library, java dot util dot regex. It adds more letters, like backslash d.

99
00:10:44,932 --> 00:10:47,385
Take the regex text a star b.

100
00:10:47,835 --> 00:11:00,742
The API has two steps. Step 1: Pattern dot compile turns the regex text into a recognizer. A recognizer is a program that scans input and checks it for the pattern.

101
00:11:01,192 --> 00:11:05,864
Step 2: a Matcher applies the pattern to a string, here a a a a a b.

102
00:11:06,314 --> 00:11:13,546
matches asks if the whole string matches. a star b matches a a a a a b, so the result is true.

103
00:11:13,996 --> 00:11:19,479
Here is the code from slide 32. The first line compiles the regex.

104
00:11:19,929 --> 00:11:26,158
The next two lines make a matcher and call matches. Compile once, then match many strings.

105
00:11:26,608 --> 00:11:33,093
Slide 32 has a warning. Here is the phone regex as it appears in Java source code.

106
00:11:33,543 --> 00:11:35,357
It has 8 backslashes.

107
00:11:35,807 --> 00:11:42,783
Java reads the string literal first. Two backslashes in the source become one backslash in the string.

108
00:11:43,233 --> 00:11:45,579
So the string has 4 backslashes.

109
00:11:46,029 --> 00:11:52,515
Only then does the regex library read the string. It sees backslash d and backslash b.

110
00:11:52,965 --> 00:11:57,871
Regexes are easy to write and hard to debug. Print the string you compile.

111
00:11:58,321 --> 00:12:04,764
Slide 33 has an exercise. There are three tokens: I D, num, and W S.

112
00:12:05,214 --> 00:12:16,777
Give a regex that recognizes an input that is a sequence of these tokens, in any order. Here W S is a token, because we recognize the whole input.

113
00:12:17,227 --> 00:12:21,664
Here is an example input: x 1, space, 42, tab, y.

114
00:12:22,114 --> 00:12:23,757
Pause the video and try it.

115
00:12:24,207 --> 00:12:31,417
Work bottom up. First cut the example into tokens: I D, W S, num, W S, I D.

116
00:12:31,867 --> 00:12:42,790
Now one regex per token. num is one or more digits: bracket zero to nine, plus. 7 and 42 match. The empty string is not a num.

117
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:52,627
W S is one white space character: space, tab, newline or carriage return. Two spaces are not one W S.

118
00:12:53,077 --> 00:13:02,122
I D starts with a letter. Then any number of letters or digits follow. So 9 lives is not an I D: it starts with a digit.

119
00:13:02,572 --> 00:13:13,175
The whole input is a sequence of tokens. Each token is an I D, a num, or a W S. So write the choice of the three, in parentheses.

120
00:13:13,625 --> 00:13:15,715
Then repeat the choice with plus.

121
00:13:16,165 --> 00:13:24,891
Plus or star? Star accepts the empty input. The slide solution uses plus, so the input has at least one token.

122
00:13:25,341 --> 00:13:31,442
Test it. x 1, space, 42, tab, y matches. Below is its cut into tokens.

123
00:13:31,892 --> 00:13:35,668
9 lives matches too: num 9, then I D lives.

124
00:13:36,118 --> 00:13:40,086
x dash 1 does not match. The dash is in no token.

125
00:13:40,536 --> 00:13:45,059
The empty input does not match. Plus needs at least one token.

126
00:13:45,509 --> 00:13:52,741
We can plug in all the definitions and get one regex. This is the solution on slide 36.

127
00:13:53,191 --> 00:14:00,657
Slide 37 changes the problem. Now I D and num tokens must be separated by white space.

128
00:14:01,107 --> 00:14:02,750
Pause the video and try it.

129
00:14:03,200 --> 00:14:07,019
First, one item. An item is an I D or a num.

130
00:14:07,469 --> 00:14:11,949
Then, zero or more times: a W S, followed by an item.

131
00:14:12,399 --> 00:14:16,047
Add an optional W S at the start and at the end.

132
00:14:16,497 --> 00:14:21,681
Here is the input: space, x 1, space, 42, space, y, space.

133
00:14:22,131 --> 00:14:24,136
The first item is x 1.

134
00:14:24,586 --> 00:14:30,005
Then two times a W S and an item: space 42, and space y.

135
00:14:30,455 --> 00:14:34,231
The spaces at the two ends are the optional W S.

136
00:14:34,681 --> 00:14:43,321
This is the solution on slide 39. The slide says the regex with all definitions plugged in is too long to write.

137
00:14:43,771 --> 00:14:47,333
Test it. With the spaces at the ends, the input matches.

138
00:14:47,783 --> 00:14:50,813
Without the spaces at the ends, it matches too.

139
00:14:51,263 --> 00:15:01,076
x 1, space, 42 y does not match. 42 y is a num followed by an I D, with no W S between them.

140
00:15:01,526 --> 00:15:12,897
x 1, two spaces, 42 does not match. W S is one character, and the regex allows exactly one W S between two items.

141
00:15:13,347 --> 00:15:17,827
The empty input does not match. There must be at least one item.

142
00:15:18,277 --> 00:15:26,981
Our note: to allow more than one white space character, use W S plus between items and W S star at the ends.

143
00:15:27,431 --> 00:15:31,697
Then two spaces match. 42 y still does not match.

144
00:15:32,147 --> 00:15:41,214
Now a past midterm question. A real constant has a decimal point, or E notation, or both. Here are five examples.

145
00:15:41,664 --> 00:15:49,088
The tilde means unary minus. It can come before the number or on the exponent. There is no unary plus.

146
00:15:49,538 --> 00:15:50,903
Write a regex.

147
00:15:51,353 --> 00:15:52,996
Pause the video and try it.

148
00:15:53,446 --> 00:15:58,481
Cut the examples into parts. Start with tilde 1 point 2 E 12.

149
00:15:58,931 --> 00:16:03,816
An optional sign: tilde, question mark. Here the sign is the tilde.

150
00:16:04,266 --> 00:16:10,389
An integer part with at least one digit: bracket zero to nine, plus. Here it is 1.

151
00:16:10,839 --> 00:16:15,404
A fraction: a point, then zero or more digits. Here it is point 2.

152
00:16:15,854 --> 00:16:21,465
An exponent: E, an optional tilde, and one or more digits. Here it is E 12.

153
00:16:21,915 --> 00:16:27,077
7 E tilde 5 has an integer part and an exponent, but no fraction.

154
00:16:27,527 --> 00:16:32,946
0 point 0 1 has an integer part and a fraction, but no exponent.

155
00:16:33,396 --> 00:16:38,687
35 point has a point and no digits after it. The question allows that.

156
00:16:39,137 --> 00:16:44,086
Here is the posted solution. First the optional sign and the integer part.

157
00:16:44,536 --> 00:16:48,397
Then a choice. Either a point, with an optional exponent.

158
00:16:48,847 --> 00:16:50,597
Or an exponent alone.

159
00:16:51,047 --> 00:16:56,956
Two optional parts would be wrong. They would accept 35, which has no point and no E.

160
00:16:57,406 --> 00:16:59,731
Test it. All five examples match.

161
00:17:00,181 --> 00:17:08,288
These do not match: 35, point 5, 1 point 2 E, plus 1 point 0, and 1 E 2 point 5.

162
00:17:08,738 --> 00:17:18,509
Our python check compares the regex with the rules of the question on all 5461 strings up to length 6.

163
00:17:18,959 --> 00:17:30,991
The next past midterm question describes an assignment statement. It has an optional label, an identifier, an equal sign, an arithmetic expression, and a semicolon.

164
00:17:31,441 --> 00:17:33,083
Pause the video and try it.

165
00:17:33,533 --> 00:17:42,515
Cut an example into its parts. Here is: loop, colon, x 1, equals, y plus z 2 times w, semicolon.

166
00:17:42,965 --> 00:17:44,821
The label is loop, colon.

167
00:17:45,271 --> 00:17:47,425
The identifier is x 1.

168
00:17:47,875 --> 00:17:49,753
Then comes the equal sign.

169
00:17:50,203 --> 00:17:57,008
Then the expression: y plus z 2 times w. The operators plus and times are inside it.

170
00:17:57,458 --> 00:17:59,634
At the end comes the semicolon.

171
00:18:00,084 --> 00:18:04,841
The posted solution names two letters: L for a letter and D for a digit.

172
00:18:05,291 --> 00:18:11,094
A label is L, L star, colon. It is optional, so write a choice with epsilon.

173
00:18:11,544 --> 00:18:15,000
An identifier is L, then L or D, star.

174
00:18:15,450 --> 00:18:21,786
An expression is an identifier, then zero or more times an operator and an identifier.

175
00:18:22,236 --> 00:18:30,855
Put the parts in order. First, the label or epsilon. Label or epsilon is the same set as label, question mark.

176
00:18:31,305 --> 00:18:34,099
Then the identifier and the equal sign.

177
00:18:34,549 --> 00:18:39,136
Then the expression and the semicolon. This is the posted solution.

178
00:18:39,586 --> 00:18:45,047
Test it with L as a small letter and D as a digit. These three statements match.

179
00:18:45,497 --> 00:18:47,865
An empty expression does not match.

180
00:18:48,315 --> 00:18:52,027
An identifier that starts with a digit does not match.

181
00:18:52,477 --> 00:18:55,080
A label with a digit does not match.

182
00:18:55,530 --> 00:18:59,306
An expression that ends with an operator does not match.

183
00:18:59,756 --> 00:19:10,657
The expression has the pattern: an item, then separator and item, star. It describes a list with separators. Exercise 2 used the same pattern.

184
00:19:11,107 --> 00:19:15,182
The last question. Write a regex for floating point numbers.

185
00:19:15,632 --> 00:19:22,437
The examples have an optional sign. The digits before the point can be missing, as in point 5.

186
00:19:22,887 --> 00:19:24,530
Pause the video and try it.

187
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:32,191
The posted solution is: an optional minus or plus, digits star, an optional point, digits star.

188
00:19:32,641 --> 00:19:34,539
All nine examples match.

189
00:19:34,989 --> 00:19:40,685
The solution also has a grading note: do not require the backslash before the point.

190
00:19:41,135 --> 00:19:50,458
But every part is optional. So the empty string, a point alone, a plus alone, and minus point also match. These are not numbers.

191
00:19:50,908 --> 00:19:56,220
Our solution: an optional sign, then a choice. Either one or more digits.

192
00:19:56,670 --> 00:20:00,083
Or digits star, a point, and one or more digits.

193
00:20:00,533 --> 00:20:02,432
All nine examples match.

194
00:20:02,882 --> 00:20:12,525
And the four bad strings do not. We checked it against Python's float on all 19531 strings up to length 6.

195
00:20:12,975 --> 00:20:22,084
To summarize. A token is a set of strings. A regex describes that set exactly, and a recognizer is generated from it.

196
00:20:22,534 --> 00:20:31,793
There are three base operations: sequence, choice and star. Question mark, plus, brackets, ranges and the dot are short forms.

197
00:20:32,243 --> 00:20:42,440
Star, plus and question mark bind tightest, then sequence, then choice. Use parentheses when you are not sure. a star is not a dot star.

198
00:20:42,890 --> 00:20:51,167
